# Example settings for Codie.
#
# Codie reads real environment variables. It does NOT read this file by itself,
# so load it into your shell first:
#
#     cp example.env .env              # then fill in the values below
#     set -a; source .env; set +a      # exports every variable in the file
#     codie check                      # shows what Codie will do; makes no request
#
# `.env` is ignored by git. Never commit a real key, and never paste one into a
# chat or an issue.

# Your OpenRouter API key (create one at https://openrouter.ai).
# Leave this empty and Codie runs in demo mode with sample answers.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# The model to ask: pick ONE of these and paste its ID after CODIE_MODEL= below.
# Each has at least one zero-data-retention (ZDR) endpoint on OpenRouter
# (checked 2026-11-08; the list changes: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/endpoints/zdr).
#
#   z-ai/glm-5.3-flash            GLM       31 ZDR providers   about $0.0005 per question
#   deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash    DeepSeek  10 ZDR providers   about $0.0003 per question
#   anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5    Haiku      2 ZDR providers   about $0.0005 per question
#
#   Also fine: deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731 (19 providers) and
#   anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5 (about 10x the price of 5.5).
#
# IMPORTANT: Codie does NOT enforce zero retention. Choosing one of these models
# does not by itself stop OpenRouter from routing you to a provider that keeps
# data. Turn ZDR on in your OpenRouter privacy settings:
#     https://openrouter.ai/settings/privacy
# Haiku caveat: its ZDR providers are Amazon Bedrock and Google, and OpenRouter's
# docs say Google Vertex endpoints are not strict ZDR since October 2026.
#
# There is deliberately no default model. Leave this empty and Codie will tell you
# to choose one once a key is set.
CODIE_MODEL=

# Optional. Defaults to OpenRouter. Only change this to point at a local test
# server. It must start with https:// (http:// is accepted for localhost only).
# CODIE_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# Optional. Folder where Ctrl+S / Ctrl+P save files, when you type a plain
# filename. A leading ~ means your home folder. It is created if missing.
# Leave unset to save in the folder you launched Codie from. A filename you type
# with a full path (/like/this.go) always goes exactly where you said.
# CODIE_SAVE_DIR=~/codie-snippets
